<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Worn obturator seven years after a right partial maxillectomy for palatal squamous cell carcinoma, with pain where it rubs, hypernasal speech, nasal leakage of liquids and weight loss, in a 74-year-old man who smokes about twenty cigars a day. Please assess and treat the prosthesis and advise on speech, swallowing and dental care. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within two weeks, on the same day as his two-week primary care visit. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Can the obturator be relined or adjusted now, and does he need a new one?</item><item>Is the sore area at the defect margin caused by the prosthesis, or does anything need a biopsy or his surgeon&apos;s review?</item><item>What can be done for his speech and the nasal leakage of liquids?</item><item>What dental and prosthesis care do you advise for his remaining teeth and the defect?</item><item>Which form of nicotine replacement works best with an obturator, and how does smoking affect the prosthesis and the tissues?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Freud, a 74-year-old physician and writer, is seen at the request of Dr. Reinholt. Seven years ago he had a right partial maxillectomy for palatal cancer, without radiation, and has worn the same obturator since. Over the past year it has loosened; liquids leak into his nose when he drinks, his speech becomes nasal as the day goes on, and a sore spot at the front of the defect hurts when he chews. He cleans the obturator by rinsing it and wears it day and night. He has smoked about fourteen cigars a day this week, down from twenty, on varenicline. He has not noticed any lump or bleeding. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Sigmund Freud was born Sigismund Schlomo Freud on May 6, 1856, in Freiberg, Moravia, now in the Czech Republic; in 1860 his family settled in Vienna. [D]</item><item>In the 1880s he experimented with cocaine himself, praised it in his paper On Coca (1884), and urged others to use it before he realized it could be addictive. [D]</item><item>According to his personal physician&apos;s account, as summarized in a 2019 review, he still used cocaine regularly in the 1890s. [D]</item><item>He began smoking in his twenties, first cigarettes and then cigars, following the example of his father, and often smoked about twenty cigars a day; his biographer described it as an addiction rather than a habit. [D]</item><item>In 1894, at 38, his friend the physician Wilhelm Fliess attributed his irregular heartbeat to smoking and told him to stop. He tried repeatedly and failed: one attempt ended within seven weeks, and a later one lasted fourteen months before he started again, describing the abstinence as torture. [D]</item><item>In 1923, at 67, he was diagnosed with cancer of the right upper jaw and palate, a squamous cell carcinoma; a first excision in April 1923 was followed by radiation treatment. [D]</item><item>Later in 1923 the oral surgeon Hans Pichler performed a radical operation that removed part of the upper jaw and soft palate, and made the obturator prosthesis that closed the defect; Freud called it &quot;the monster.&quot; Pichler provided most of his later prosthetic care. [D]</item><item>He kept smoking cigars after the cancer was diagnosed and refused to give them up. [D]</item><item>At 73, after a heart illness, he stopped smoking for 23 days and then gradually started again. [D]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

