    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Worn obturator with a poor seal (Z90.09). After seven years the acrylic is worn and porous and the tissues have changed, so the bulb no longer seals at the back. A chairside reline with a soft tissue conditioner restores the seal for now, but a new definitive obturator is needed. [S]</item><item>Pressure ulcer at the defect margin (Z90.09). A shallow ulcer under an overextended flange, with no induration or mass, is a pressure ulcer. The flange was relieved today. It should heal within two weeks; if it does not, his head and neck surgeon should see it and take a biopsy, because new or persistent lesions in a smoker with a past oral cancer may be a recurrence or a second primary. [S]</item><item>Hypernasal speech and nasal leakage (R49.21). Both come from the poor seal and improved at once with the reline. A short course of speech-language therapy, starting now with the relined obturator and continuing after the new one is fitted, will help him use it well. [S]</item><item>Dental care, nicotine replacement and smoking. He has two areas of root caries and a high caries risk. Smoking stains and roughens the acrylic, encourages Candida, slows healing and raises the risk of a second cancer in the mouth. Nicotine gum sticks to acrylic, so a lozenge, dissolved on the side away from the defect, is better; the patch has no effect on the prosthesis. No radiation was given, so there is no risk of osteoradionecrosis and extractions can be done in the usual way if needed. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Reinholt&apos;s intake note and today&apos;s two-week note. Weight 58.2 kg today, from 58.4 kg; albumin 3.4 g/dL and hemoglobin 13.2 g/dL at intake. Surveillance letter from his head and neck surgeon ten months ago: no recurrence. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Chairside reline with a soft tissue conditioner and relief of the anterior flange done today. [S]</item><item>New definitive obturator: impressions in about four weeks and fitting about six weeks later. [S]</item><item>Recheck the ulcer in two weeks; his head and neck surgeon and a biopsy if it has not healed. [S]</item><item>Speech-language therapy sessions to improve resonance and swallowing with the relined and then the new obturator. [S]</item><item>Brush the obturator twice daily, soak it overnight in a denture cleanser, and leave it out for at least six hours each night once the new one fits well. [S]</item><item>High-fluoride toothpaste at night, treatment of the two root caries, and dental cleaning every three months. [S]</item><item>Nicotine lozenge rather than gum, and the patch, are both suitable with the obturator; stopping smoking will protect the tissues and the prosthesis. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Reinholt, who sees him the same day. The plan was explained to Mr. Freud, who agreed and chose to have the impressions taken before his summer schedule. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Prosthodontics clinic for impressions in about four weeks and fitting of the new obturator about six weeks later, then a check one month after fitting and yearly; Dr. Reinholt continues tobacco treatment, pain care and survivorship care. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
